CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure shows a physical analogy for the curl of a vector field at a point. At the center is a labeled point P0, around which concentric dashed blue arcs indicate a circular fluid flow pattern. Four paddle-like rectangular surfaces (salmon-colored) radiate outward from P0 like propeller blades. A vertical spindle passes through P0, and the vector Curl F(P0) labels an upward arrow along the spindle, with a curved arrow showing the direction of rotation. The figure provides the physical interpretation that a small paddle wheel immersed in the fluid at P0 rotates most rapidly when its spindle is aligned with curl F(P0), establishing the connection between the curl vector and the direction of maximum circulation density.

PROJECTION TYPE: perspective
Use the scaffold's true perspective projection after building all geometry. Use this only when the source has real vanishing-point convergence or distance-based size shrinkage; then depth should visibly affect apparent size.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - central point P0 drawn as a small black sphere with label 'P0'
  - vertical spindle (thin dark cylinder) passing through P0 along the world up-axis
  - curl vector arrow pointing up along the spindle, labeled 'Curl F(P0)' above it
  - curved rotation arrow wrapping around the upper spindle, indicating counterclockwise spin viewed from above
  - four salmon-colored rectangular paddle blades radiating from P0 at 90-degree intervals, each pitched like a propeller blade
  - three concentric circular flow arcs (light blue, broken/dashed) lying in the horizontal plane around the field center, representing the swirling fluid
  - small arrowheads on the flow arcs showing the counterclockwise circulation direction

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
World: Y is up; the flow plane is the XZ plane at y = 0, scene spans about ±4.5 in X/Z and −0.5 to 2.5 in Y. Flow field is FIXED in world space, centered at the origin: three concentric broken circular arcs, radii 1.6, 2.4, 3.2, drawn as ~270-degree light-blue arcs (#6fb3d8, tube radius ~0.03) with small arrowheads indicating counterclockwise flow viewed from +Y. The paddle-wheel assembly is a single draggable Group whose origin is P0 (default at world origin): black sphere radius 0.12 labeled 'P0'; spindle = dark cylinder radius 0.035 from y = −0.25 to y = 1.5; curl arrow = shaft radius 0.05 plus cone head starting at y = 0, pointing +Y, label 'Curl F(P0)' floating above its tip; curved rotation arrow = black torus-arc radius 0.38 around the spindle at y ~ 1.55 with an arrowhead, CCW from above. Four blades: boxes 1.05 (radial length) x 0.02 x 0.55, centers at radius 0.62, azimuths 0/90/180/270 degrees, each pitched 30 degrees about its radial axis so the flow catches them; salmon fill #f2c1a7 with thin dark (#4a3a32) edges. Math for correctness: velocity field F(x,z) = S * exp(-(x^2+z^2)/8) * <−z, 0, x>, where S is the rotation-rate slider; curl at P0 is c = S * exp(-r^2/8) * (2 − r^2/4) in the +Y direction, zero at r = 2*sqrt(2) ~ 2.83, negative beyond. Set curl arrow length = 0.6*|c| (flip to −Y when c < 0) and paddle angular speed Ω = c/2 (sign included) so arrow length and spin speed always agree.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "orbit",
    "type": "orbit",
    "teaches": "The curl vector points along the paddle wheel's spin axis (right-hand rule); orbiting confirms the blades lie in the flow plane while Curl F(P0) is perpendicular to it"
  },
  {
    "id": "dragPoint",
    "type": "drag",
    "teaches": "Curl is a local property of the field: dragging P0 away from the vortex center shrinks the curl vector, the paddle stops where the local circulation vanishes (r ~ 2.8), and beyond that the curl flips sign so the arrow points down and the wheel spins clockwise"
  },
  {
    "id": "rotationRate",
    "type": "slider",
    "label": "Rotation rate",
    "range": [
      0,
      3,
      0.1
    ],
    "default": 1.5,
    "teaches": "The magnitude of curl F at P0 equals twice the angular speed of the paddle wheel: raising the flow's rotation rate makes the blades spin faster and the curl arrow grow proportionally"
  }
]

DEMO STEPS (tween through these using goToStep(); each step drives the controls above):
[
  {
    "title": "Paddle wheel in the vortex",
    "narration": "At the field center the swirling flow catches the pitched blades and the wheel spins counterclockwise. The curl vector points straight up along the spindle — the axis of maximum circulation.",
    "state": {
      "rotationRate": 1.5
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Weak circulation",
    "narration": "Lowering the rotation rate makes the paddle creep and the Curl F(P0) arrow shrink: curl measures the density of circulation, not the flow's speed at one point.",
    "state": {
      "rotationRate": 0.4
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Strong circulation",
    "narration": "At a high rotation rate the blades whirl and the curl arrow stretches upward — its length is twice the paddle's angular speed.",
    "state": {
      "rotationRate": 2.5
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Drag P0 off-center",
    "narration": "Now drag P0 outward across the fixed flow pattern. The spin slows, stops entirely near r = 2.8 where the local curl is zero, then reverses — the arrow flips downward and the wheel turns clockwise.",
    "state": {
      "rotationRate": 1.5
    }
  }
]

CAMERA VIEW (framing only). Call setCameraView({ projection, ...these }) AFTER all geometry is added. Keep the projection MECHANISM from PROJECTION TYPE above (orthographic / sheared-oblique / perspective). For perspective these params set the pose (azimuth/elevation/roll); for the two parallel projections pose is a standard default (see PROJECTION TYPE above), and these params set only roll and framing (heightFraction):
{
  "azimuthDeg": 45,
  "elevationDeg": 22,
  "rollDeg": 0,
  "heightFraction": 0.65
}

NOTES: Structure the scene as: static flow-arc group at world origin + draggable paddle group. Implement dragPoint by raycasting the pointer onto the y = 0 plane and clamping the drag radius to <= 4; move the whole paddle group (sphere, spindle, blades, curl arrow, curved arrow) to the new P0. Each frame, recompute c from P0's XZ position and the slider value, then rotate the blade sub-group about local Y by Omega*dt and rescale/reorient the curl arrow (scale.y and 180-degree flip when c < 0); spin pauses automatically when the slider is 0. Render flow arcs as TubeGeometry arcs with gaps rather than dashed-line hacks so they read as the broken blue strokes in the source. Use HTML/sprite labels for 'P0' and 'Curl F(P0)' that always face the camera; keep the curved rotation arrow hidden or spinning with the blades for extra clarity.

